{"name":"Indolence","hex":"#a29dad","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"indolence","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Habitual laziness or sloth.","Lack of pain in a tumour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/indolence"}],"entries":[{"title":"Laziness","blocks":[{"text":"Laziness is emotional disinclination to activity or exertion despite having the ability to act or to\nexert oneself. It is often used as a pejorative; terms for a person seen to be lazy include \"couch potato\" and \"slacker\". Related concepts include sloth, a Christian sin, abulia, a medical term for reduced motivation, and lethargy, a state of lacking energy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/British_club_scene.jpg/330px-British_club_scene.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laziness"},{"title":"Benign tumor","blocks":[{"text":"A benign tumor is a mass of cells (tumor) that does not invade neighboring tissue or metastasize. Compared to malignant (cancerous) tumors, benign tumors generally have a slower growth rate. Benign tumors have relatively well differentiated cells. They are often surrounded by an outer surface or stay contained within the epithelium. Common examples of benign tumors include moles and uterine fibroids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Normal_Epidermis_and_Dermis_with_Intradermal_Nevus_10x.JPG/330px-Normal_Epidermis_and_Dermis_with_Intradermal_Nevus_10x.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benign_tumor"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Familial_adenomatous_polyposis_as_seen_on_sigmoidoscopy.jpg/500px-Familial_adenomatous_polyposis_as_seen_on_sigmoidoscopy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Familial_adenomatous_polyposis_as_seen_on_sigmoidoscopy.jpg/500px-Familial_adenomatous_polyposis_as_seen_on_sigmoidoscopy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Endoscopic image of sigmoid colon of a patient with familial adenomatous polyposis","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFamilial_adenomatous_polyposis_as_seen_on_sigmoidoscopy.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Types_of_tumor_cells.jpg/500px-Types_of_tumor_cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Types_of_tumor_cells.jpg/1280px-Types_of_tumor_cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Types_of_tumor_cells.jpg/500px-Types_of_tumor_cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Benign (L) vs Malignant tumor (R)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATypes_of_tumor_cells.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/BENIGN_AND_MALIGNANT_TUMOUR.png/500px-BENIGN_AND_MALIGNANT_TUMOUR.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/BENIGN_AND_MALIGNANT_TUMOUR.png/500px-BENIGN_AND_MALIGNANT_TUMOUR.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Diagram showing two epithelial tumors. The upper tumor is a benign tumor that is non-invasive. Benign tumors are usually round in shape and encapsulated by fibrous connective tissue. The lower picture depicts a malignant tumor. It is irregularly shaped, vascular, and it is invasive, crossing the basement membrane.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABENIGN_AND_MALIGNANT_TUMOUR.png"}],"sounds":{"indolence":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wi